* Input-driven waveform coefficient control
* This follows the SPISim free-spice waveform flow: threshold IN, use a
* short T-line for one-step edge differentiation, latch the scaled edge
* time through another T-line, and evaluate Ku/Kd versus elapsed ns.
* Smooth sigmoid replaces hard ? : thresholds to prevent Newton-Raphson
* oscillation at boundary crossings (root cause of attosecond timestep trap).
* tanh(92*x): transition width ~0.6mV, sharp enough physically, smooth for Newton.
* B14 uses sqrt(x^2+eps) instead of abs(x), avoids the non-differentiable kink at x=0.
B10 NINX 0 V={0.5 + 0.5*tanh(20*(V(IN,VSS) - {input_threshold}))}
B11 NENABLE 0 V={0.5 + 0.5*tanh(20*(V(EN,VSS) - {enable_threshold}))}
B12 NI 0 V={V(NINX) - 0.5}
B13 N2 0 V={V(NI,N9) * 8}
* Smooth abs: sqrt(x^2 + eps) instead of abs(x), avoids the kink at x=0
B14 N3 0 V={sqrt(V(N2)*V(N2) + 1e-6)}
B15 N4 0 V={tanh(20*(V(N3) - 0.5))}
* N5 = time (always). Constant slope = no LTE penalty, no jump at edge start.
* The original '(N4>0)?time:0' caused 210V jump at t=210ns edge -> Newton failure.
B16 N5 0 V={time*{time_scale}}
* N6 latches N5 during transition (N4>0), holds N8 otherwise.
B17 N6 0 V={(0.5 + 0.5*tanh(20*V(N4))) * V(N5) + (0.5 - 0.5*tanh(20*V(N4))) * V(N8)}
* Cap NX at table maximum (5.96 ns) so it stops being time-variant in steady
* state.  Smooth the N6>=1 threshold with tanh to avoid hard jump in NX.
* N6 crosses 1.0 when the edge is well-established: tanh(92*(N6-1.0)) smooth gate.
B18 NX 0 V={min(5.96, (0.5+0.5*tanh(20*(V(N6)+V(N8)-0.001))) * (time*{time_scale} - V(N8)))}
T1 N6 0 N8 0 Z0=50 Td={edge_delay}
T2 NI 0 N9 0 Z0=50 Td={edge_delay}
R5 N8 0 50
R6 N9 0 50

